    latestPredictions,
    recentUsers,
    userGrowth
] = await Promise.all([
Notice we've added:

userGrowth
to the destructuring.

Then remove this entire block:

const userGrowth = await User.aggregate([
    ...
]);
and instead place the aggregation as the last item inside the Promise.all() array:

User.aggregate([

    {
        $group: {

            _id: {

                year: { $year: "$createdAt" },

                month: { $month: "$createdAt" }

            },

            total: {

                $sum: 1

            }

        }

    },

    {
        $sort: {

            "_id.year": 1,

            "_id.month": 1

        }

    }

])
Notice:

No const

No await

Just another promise in the array.

Now MongoDB performs all 13 queries in parallel.

Why am I insisting on this now?
Because the next chart (Prediction Trends) will also use an aggregation.

If we leave aggregations outside Promise.all(), eventually we'll have:

Promise.all()

↓

Aggregate

↓

Aggregate

↓

Aggregate

↓

Aggregate
which becomes inefficient.

Putting them all into Promise.all() now keeps the controller scalable.

