🚀 Now we're making our first administrative update.
This is the moment where the admin can actually change a user's privileges.

Step 1 — Add the POST route
In routes/adminRoutes.js, add:

router.post(
    "/users/:id",
    authMiddleware.isLoggedIn,
    roleMiddleware.allowRoles(
        ROLES.SUPERUSER,
        ROLES.ADMIN
    ),
    adminController.updateUser
);
Step 2 — Create the controller
In controllers/adminController.js, add:

exports.updateUser = async (req, res, next) => {

    try {

        const {

            role,

            subscriptionStatus

        } = req.body;

        const user = await User.findById(req.params.id);

        if (!user) {

            return res.status(404).render("404", {

                pageTitle: "User Not Found"

            });

        }

        user.role = role;

        user.subscriptionStatus = subscriptionStatus;

        await user.save();

        res.redirect("/admin/users");

    } catch (err) {

        next(err);

    }

};
What this does
When you click Save Changes:

Form
      │
POST /admin/users/:id
      │
updateUser()
      │
Find User
      │
Update Role
Update Subscription
      │
Save MongoDB
      │
Redirect back to Manage Users
Exactly the same pattern we've used elsewhere in the project.

Test
Here's the test I'd like you to perform:

Open a user.

Change User → Admin (or vice versa).

Click Save Changes.

Return to Manage Users.

You should immediately see the role badge updated.

Then try:

