Excellent. This is one of those classic EJS gotchas, and the error message tells us exactly what's happening.

Why it happened
In EJS, this expression:

<%= details || "Default text" %>
only works if details exists.

If details was never passed to the view, EJS throws:

ReferenceError: details is not defined
Unlike plain JavaScript objects, EJS doesn't automatically treat missing variables as undefined.

Solution 1 (Recommended): Always pass details
Whenever you render 403.ejs, include a details property, even if it's just an empty string.

For example, in your deleteUser controller:

return res.status(403).render("403", {
    pageTitle: "Access Denied",
    message: "You cannot delete your own account.",
    details: "For security reasons, administrators cannot delete the account they are currently signed in with."
});
And in every other place you render 403.ejs, pass:

return res.status(403).render("403", {
    pageTitle: "Access Denied",
    message: "Sorry, you don't have permission to access this page.",
    details: ""
});
Solution 2 (My Preferred Long-Term Approach)
Since you'll probably use this page in many places, I would make the variables available by default before rendering.

Instead of relying on details always existing, change the EJS to check whether the variable exists first.

Replace:

<%= details || "If you believe this is an error, please contact the administrator or log in with an account that has the required permissions." %>
with:

<%= typeof details !== "undefined"
    ? details
    : "If you believe this is an error, please contact the administrator or log in with an account that has the required permissions."
%>
Do the same for message:

<%= typeof message !== "undefined"
    ? message
    : "Sorry, you don't have permission to access this page."
%>
And for the title:

<%= typeof pageTitle !== "undefined"
    ? pageTitle
    : "403 - Access Denied"
%>
This way, your page becomes bulletproof. It works whether or not the controller passes those variables.

My recommendation for Prof Predict
I'd go with Solution 2.

Why?

Because your 403.ejs is becoming a shared error template. As your application grows, there will inevitably be places where you forget to pass details or message. By using typeof, the template becomes self-sufficient and won't crash just because a variable wasn't provided.

Let's do one thing first
Before we continue with the "last Superuser" protection, paste your 403.ejs after you update it with the typeof checks (or let me know you've made the change). Once that's working and the self-deletion page displays the custom message correctly, we'll move on to the next safeguard. That way, we're building on a solid foundation instead of carrying a fragile template forward.


