This is perfect timing. 😊

Your global error handler is functional, but now that we've upgraded the error pages, it's time to upgrade the handler too.

Right now you have:

//Global Error Handler
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
    console.log("Global error handler");
    console.error(err);

    res.status(500).render("500", {
        pageTitle: "Error",
        message: err.message
    });
});
It works, but it's only passing two values.

I recommend replacing it with this
// Global Error Handler
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {

    console.error("Global Error Handler:");
    console.error(err);

    res.status(err.status || 500).render("500", {

        pageTitle: "500 - Internal Server Error",

        message:
            process.env.NODE_ENV === "production"
                ? "Oops! Something went wrong while processing your request."
                : err.message,

        details:
            process.env.NODE_ENV === "production"
                ? "Please try again later. If the problem persists, contact our support team."
                : "An unexpected error occurred while processing this request.",

        error:
            process.env.NODE_ENV === "production"
                ? null
                : err

    });

});
Why this is better
Development (NODE_ENV=development)
If an error occurs:

ReferenceError

prediction is not defined

Development Error Details
ReferenceError...
Stack Trace...
Exactly what we want while building.

Production (NODE_ENV=production)
Visitors see:

500 - Internal Server Error

Oops! Something went wrong while processing your request.

Please try again later.
No stack trace.

No internal filenames.

No line numbers.

That's much safer.

Another improvement
Instead of:

console.log("Global error handler");
console.error(err);
I changed it to:

console.error("Global Error Handler:");
console.error(err);
This makes your terminal output much easier to scan.

Example:

Global Error Handler:

ReferenceError:
prediction is not defined

at predictionController.js:212
...
One tiny improvement I'd make
Sometimes you'll deliberately throw errors with a status code:

const error = new Error("Prediction not found");

error.status = 404;

next(error);
Our handler already supports this:

res.status(err.status || 500)
But right now, it always renders 500.ejs, even for a 404.

A more scalable pattern is:

if (err.status === 404) {
    return res.status(404).render("404", {
        pageTitle: "404 - Page Not Found",
        message: err.message,
        details: err.details
    });
}

if (err.status === 403) {
    return res.status(403).render("403", {
        pageTitle: "403 - Access Denied",
        message: err.message,
        details: err.details
    });
}

// Otherwise render 500
That gives you one centralized error handler that can render the correct page based on the error status, instead of every controller having to decide which error template to use.

I think that's a great direction for Prof Predict
It's cleaner because controllers only need to create an error and pass it to next(err). The global error handler becomes the single place responsible for deciding which page to render.

As Prof Predict grows—with prediction management, payments, subscriptions, and more routes—that centralized approach will keep your controllers focused on business logic while all error presentation stays in one place. That's the pattern I'd recommend adopting as we continue building the application.


