            process.env.NODE_ENV === "production"
                ? null
                : err

    });

});
Why this is better
Now your controllers can simply do:

const err = new Error("You cannot delete your own account.");

err.status = 403;

err.details = "For security reasons, administrators cannot delete the account they are currently signed in with.";

next(err);
No rendering.

No duplicated code.

The global error handler decides which page to render.

The same applies for:

err.status = 404;
or

err.status = 500;
⭐ This is a significant architectural improvement
Earlier in the project, each controller rendered its own error pages. That works, but it spreads presentation logic across your codebase.

With the pattern above:

Controllers focus on application logic and raise errors.

The global error handler decides how to present those errors.

The EJS templates display them consistently.

That's a cleaner separation of responsibilities and will make Prof Predict easier to maintain as it grows with prediction management, subscriptions, and payment integration.


