err.status = 403;

err.details = "For security reasons, administrators cannot delete the account they are currently signed in with.";

next(err);

Exactly! 👍 That's the pattern we're moving toward.

There's just one small improvement I'd make.

Instead of:

const err = new Error("You cannot delete your own account.");

err.status = 403;

err.details = "For security reasons, administrators cannot delete the account they are currently signed in with.";

next(err);
I'd write:

const err = new Error("You cannot delete your own account.");

err.status = 403;

err.details =
    "For security reasons, administrators cannot delete the account they are currently signed in with.";

return next(err);
Why return next(err)?
Using return ensures the function exits immediately. Without it, if someone later adds more code after next(err), that code could still execute, leading to unexpected behavior such as trying to send another response.

For example:

if (req.session.user._id.toString() === req.params.id) {

    const err = new Error("You cannot delete your own account.");

    err.status = 403;

    err.details =
        "For security reasons, administrators cannot delete the account they are currently signed in with.";

    return next(err);

}

// Execution only reaches here if the user is allowed to be deleted.
This makes the intent crystal clear.

Your deleteUser should now look like this
exports.deleteUser = async (req, res, next) => {

    try {

        if (req.session.user._id.toString() === req.params.id) {

            const err = new Error("You cannot delete your own account.");

            err.status = 403;

            err.details =
                "For security reasons, administrators cannot delete the account they are currently signed in with.";

            return next(err);

        }

        const user = await User.findById(req.params.id);

        if (!user) {

            const err = new Error("User not found.");

            err.status = 404;

            err.details =
                "The requested account may have already been removed.";

            return next(err);

        }

        await User.findByIdAndDelete(req.params.id);

        return res.redirect("/admin/users");

    } catch (err) {

        return next(err);

    }

};
Notice the consistency:

return next(err); for errors.

return res.redirect(...); for successful completion.

That consistency makes the flow of the controller very easy to follow.

Test checklist
Once you've updated it, verify these four scenarios:

✅ Delete another user → User is deleted and you're redirected to /admin/users.

✅ Try to delete yourself → You see the dynamic 403 page with the custom message.

✅ Visit a non-existent route (e.g. /does-not-exist) → You see the dynamic 404 page.

✅ Trigger a server error intentionally (later, during testing) → You see the dynamic 500 page.

If all four pass, we'll have a solid centralized error-handling foundation in place and can move on to protecting the last Superuser, which is the next important safeguard. 🚀


