Excellent. 😊

You've just crossed a line that many developers don't realize exists.

Before today, we were building features.

Now we're building a system.

Phase 2 — Protect the Last Superuser
This is one of those features users will hopefully never notice—but it's incredibly important.

Imagine this database:

Username	Role
Rudolph	superuser
Someone clicks Delete.

Now there are zero Superusers.

Who's going to:

Manage users?

Approve subscriptions?

Create predictions?

Fix problems?

Nobody.

That's why production systems almost always protect the last administrator account.

Step 1
In deleteUser(), after you've found the user:

const user = await User.findById(req.params.id);
and after the "User not found" check, add this block:

if (user.role === "superuser") {

    const superUserCount = await User.countDocuments({

        role: "superuser"

    });

    if (superUserCount === 1) {

        const err = new Error(
            "The last Superuser cannot be deleted."
        );

        err.status = 403;

        err.details =
            "At least one Superuser account must remain in the system.";

        return next(err);

    }

}
Your controller flow now becomes
Delete Request
       │
       ▼
Is it your own account?
       │
   Yes ▼ No
  403 Error
       │
       ▼
Find User
       │
       ▼
User exists?
       │
 No ▼ Yes
404 Error
       │
       ▼
Is user a Superuser?
       │
 No ▼ Yes
Delete User
       │
       ▼
How many Superusers exist?
       │
   >1 ▼ =1
 Delete   403 Error
Notice how the validation flows from the most critical checks down to the delete operation.

Why I like checking after finding the user
If we counted Superusers first and the user ID didn't exist, we'd perform an unnecessary database query.

Your current order is more efficient:

Check self-deletion.

Find the user.

Verify the user exists.

If they're a Superuser, count Superusers.

Delete.

Only one extra query is made, and only when it's actually needed.

Test Cases
Once you've added it, try these scenarios:

Scenario 1
One Superuser in the database.

Click Delete.

Expected:

403

The last Superuser cannot be deleted.

At least one Superuser account must remain in the system.
Scenario 2
Create a second Superuser.

Delete one of them.

Expected:

Deleted successfully.
One Superuser still remains.

Scenario 3
Delete a normal user.

Expected:

Deleted successfully.
No effect on Superuser protection.

What comes next (my favorite part)
Once this is working, we move from backend safety to frontend polish:

🗑️ Bootstrap Confirmation Modal

Instead of:

Delete
↓
Gone.
We'll have:

Delete
↓
─────────────────────────────
Delete User

Username: johndoe

This action cannot be undone.

[ Cancel ]   [ Delete ]
─────────────────────────────
That dramatically reduces accidental deletions and makes the admin panel feel much more professional.

I also want to point out something I'm happy about: we're not just checking boxes on a roadmap anymore. Every new feature is being built with reliability in mind. That's the difference between an app that works and one that's ready for real administrators to use every day. 🚀


