Excellent. This is exactly why I asked to see it.

At the moment, your Global Error Handler always renders 500.ejs, regardless of whether the error is a 403, 404, or an actual server error.

So when your controller does:

const err = new Error("You cannot delete your own account.");

err.status = 403;

err.details =
    "For security reasons, administrators cannot delete the account they are currently signed in with.";

return next(err);
your Global Error Handler receives it—but then ignores err.status and renders 500.ejs.

Replace your Global Error Handler with this
// Global Error Handler
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {

    console.error("Global Error Handler:");
    console.error(err);

    const status = err.status || 500;

    // Handle 403 and 404 with the reusable error page
    if (status === 403 || status === 404) {

        return res.status(status).render("404", {

            pageTitle:
                status === 403
                    ? "403 - Access Denied"
                    : "404 - Page Not Found",

            message: err.message,

            details: err.details

        });

    }

    // Handle all other server errors
    return res.status(500).render("500", {

        pageTitle: "500 - Internal Server Error",

        message:
            process.env.NODE_ENV === "production"
                ? "Oops! Something went wrong while processing your request."
                : err.message,

        details:
            process.env.NODE_ENV === "production"
                ? "Please try again later. If the problem persists, contact our support team."
                : "An unexpected error occurred while processing this request.",

        error:
            process.env.NODE_ENV === "production"
                ? null
                : err

    });

});
